== Chapter XI

#block(inset: (x: 1em))[
  #set par(justify: false)
  #text(size: 0.9em)[The working of sin by deceit to entangle the affections. The ways it is done. Means of preventing it.]
]

#smallcaps[The] second thing the apostle’s words ascribe to the #emph[deceitful working of sin] is its #emph[enticing.] A man is drawn away and #emph[enticed.] This seems to concern the #emph[affections] in particular, as drawing away concerns the mind. The #emph[mind] is drawn away from duty, and the #emph[affections] are enticed to sin. When this prevails, a man is said to be #emph[enticed,] or #emph[entangled as with a bait,] as the word means. For there is an allusion in it to the #emph[bait] by which a fish is caught on the hook that holds it to its destruction. Concerning this effect of the deceit of sin, we will briefly show two things.

First, what it is to be #emph[enticed,] or #emph[entangled] with the bait of sin, to have the #emph[affections] tainted with an inclination to it, and when they are so.

Secondly, what course sin takes, and what way it goes on in, to entice, ensnare, or entangle the soul in this way.

For the first: first, the affections are certainly #emph[entangled] when they stir up #emph[frequent imaginations] about the #emph[object set before them,] toward which this deceit of sin leads and entices. When sin prevails and the affections have gone fully after it, it #emph[fills the imagination] with it, continually possessing it with images, likenesses, and appearances of it. Such persons “devise iniquity” and plot evil on their beds, and they carry it out when they are able, when “the power is in their hands” (Mic 2:1). In particular, #emph[Peter] tells us that they “have eyes full of an adulteress, and they cannot cease from sin” (2 Pet 2:14). That is, their #emph[imaginations] are possessed with a continual representation of the object of their lusts. And it is so #emph[in part] where the affections are #emph[in part] entangled with sin and begin to turn aside to it. #emph[John] tells us that the things in the world are “the lust of the flesh, the lust of the eyes, and the pride of life” (1 John 2:16). “The lust of the eyes” is what is conveyed to the soul by the eyes. Now what is meant is not the #emph[bodily sense] of #emph[seeing,] but the fixing of the imagination on such things through that sense. It is called the #emph[eyes] because by the imagination things are constantly represented to the mind and soul, as outward objects are represented to the inward sense by the eyes. And often the #emph[outward sight of the eyes] is the occasion of these imaginations. So #emph[Achan] declares how sin prevailed with him (Josh 7:21). First #emph[he saw] the bar of gold and the #emph[cloak from Babylon,] and then he coveted them. He turned over their pleasures and their profit in his imagination, and then fixed his heart on getting them. Now the heart may have a settled, fixed detestation of sin. Yet if a man finds that the #emph[imagination] of his mind is frequently solicited by it and occupied with it, he may know that his #emph[affections] are secretly enticed and entangled.

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[Secondly, this entanglement is heightened when the #emph[imagination] can prevail with the mind #emph[to lodge vain thoughts] in it with secret delight and #emph[pleasure.] #emph[Casuists] call this #emph[cogitatio morosa cum delectation,] a lingering thought with delight, which toward forbidden objects is in all cases #emph[actually] sinful. And yet this may happen when the #emph[consent of the will] to sin has not been obtained, when the soul would not for the world do the thing about which thoughts nevertheless begin to lodge in the mind. The prophet complains of this #emph[lodging of vain thoughts] in the heart as something greatly sinful and to be abhorred (Jer 4:14). All these thoughts are #emph[messengers] that carry sin back and forth between the #emph[imagination] and the #emph[affections,] and they keep increasing it, inflaming the imagination and entangling the affections more and more. #emph[Achan] thinks about the #emph[bar of gold]\; this makes him like it and love it. By loving it his thoughts are infected, and they return to imagining its worth and fine appearance; and so little by little the soul is #emph[inflamed] to sin. And here, if the #emph[will] gives up its #emph[sovereignty,] sin is actually conceived.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[Thirdly, inclinations or readiness to listen to #emph[excuses that lessen sin,] or to the reliefs offered against sin once it is committed, show that the affections are entangled with it. We have shown, and will show further still, that it is a great part of the deceit of sin to offer the mind thoughts that lessen and extenuate sin. “Is it not a little one?” or, there is mercy provided; or, it will be left off and given up in due time: this is its language in a deceived heart. Now when there is a readiness in the soul to listen to and welcome such secret suggestions arising from this deceit, with regard to any sin or any course it cannot approve, it is evidence that the #emph[affections] are enticed. When the soul is willing, as it were, to be tempted, to be courted by sin, to listen to its flirtations and solicitations, it has lost some of its marriage affection for Christ and is entangled. This is “gazing at wine while it is red, when it sparkles in the cup and goes down smoothly” (Prov 23:31): a pleasing contemplation of the invitations of sin, whose end the wise man gives us in verse 32. When the deceit of sin has prevailed this far on any person, then he is #emph[enticed] or #emph[entangled.] The #emph[will] has not yet come to the actual conception of this or that sin by its consent, but the whole soul is close to inclining to it. I could give many other instances as tokens and evidences of this entanglement. These may be enough to show what we mean by it.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[Our next inquiry is how, or by what means, the #emph[deceit of sin] goes about enticing and entangling the affections in this way. Two or three of its baits are plain here.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[First, it makes use of the way it has already prevailed on the mind, in #emph[drawing it away] from its watch and circumspection. The wise man says in Prov 1:17: “How futile it is to spread the net where any bird can see it!” or, as in the #emph[original,] before the eyes of everything that has a wing. If the bird has eyes open to see the snare, and a wing to carry it away, it will not be caught. So the #emph[deceit of sin] would spread its #emph[snares] and #emph[nets] in vain to entangle the soul, as long as the eyes of the mind are intent on what it is doing, and so stir up the wings of the soul’s #emph[will] and #emph[affections] to carry it away and escape. But if the #emph[eyes] are put out or diverted, the #emph[wings] are of very little use for escape. That is why this is one of the ways used by those who catch #emph[birds] or fowl in their nets: they have false lights, or appearances of things, to divert the sight of their prey, and when that is done they seize the moment to cast their nets over them. So the deceit of sin does. As has been shown, it first #emph[draws away] and diverts the mind by false reasonings and pretenses, and then it casts its net over the affections to entangle them.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[Secondly, taking advantage of such seasons, it sets sin before the soul as #emph[desirable,] as exceedingly satisfying to the corrupt part of our affections. It gilds over the object with a #emph[thousand pretenses,] which it presents to corrupt lustings. This is the #emph[laying of a bait,] to which the apostle plainly alludes in this verse. A #emph[bait] is something desirable and suitable set before the hungry creature to satisfy it, and it is made desirable and suitable by every kind of trick. This is how sin is presented to the soul with the help of the imagination; that is, sinful and inordinate objects, which the #emph[affections] cling to, are presented this way. The apostle tells us that there are “pleasures of sin” (Heb 11:25), and unless they are despised, as they were by #emph[Moses,] there is no escaping sin itself. So those who #emph[live in sin] are said to “live in pleasure” (Jas 5:5). Now this pleasure of sin consists in its #emph[suitableness] to give satisfaction to the flesh, to lust, to corrupt affections. That is the reason for the warning in Rom 13:14: “make no provision for the lusts of the flesh.” That is, do not let your minds, thoughts, or affections fix on sinful objects suited to satisfy the lusts of the flesh, so as to nourish and cherish those lusts by them. He speaks to the same purpose again in Gal 5:16: “do not gratify the lusts of the flesh.” Do not bring in the pleasures of sin to satisfy them. When men are under the power of sin, they are said to be “fulfilling the cravings of the flesh and indulging its desires and thoughts” (Eph 2:3). So the #emph[deceit of sin] tries to entangle the #emph[affections] by setting before them, with the help of the #emph[imagination,] the suitableness that is in sin to satisfy their corrupt lusts, which are now set at some liberty by the inattention of the mind. It presents its #emph[wine as sparkling in the cup,] the beauty of the adulteress, the riches of the world to sensual and covetous persons, and something of the same kind, in some degree, to believers themselves. So when sin would entangle the soul, I say, it prevails with the #emph[imagination] to solicit the heart by representing this false, painted beauty, or the pretended satisfaction of sin. And then, if Satan comes in to its help with some special temptation, it often inflames all the affections and throws the whole soul into disorder.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[Thirdly, it #emph[hides the danger] that goes with sin. It covers it as the #emph[hook] is covered with the #emph[bait,] or as the net is spread over with food for the bird to be caught. It is indeed not possible for sin to deprive the soul utterly of the knowledge of its #emph[danger.] It cannot rid the soul of its notion or persuasion that “the wages of sin is death,” and that it is “God’s righteous decree that those who do such things are worthy of death.” But this it will do: it will so take up and possess the mind and affections with the baits and desirableness of sin that it diverts them from an actual and practical contemplation of its danger. What #emph[Satan] did in and by his first temptation, sin has done ever since. At first #emph[Eve] guards herself by calling to mind the #emph[danger of sin:] if we eat it or touch it, we will die (Gen 3:3). But as soon as #emph[Satan] had filled her mind with the beauty of the fruit and its usefulness to make one wise, how quickly she laid aside her practical, prevailing consideration of the danger of eating it and of the curse due to it! Or else she relieves herself with a vain hope and pretense that it would not come about, because the #emph[serpent] told her so. So #emph[David] was beguiled by the deceit of sin in his great transgression. Since his lust was pleased and satisfied, the consideration of the guilt and danger of his transgression was taken away. That is why he is said to have “despised the Lord” (2 Sam 12:9): he did not consider the evil that was in his heart, and the danger that went with it in the threatening, or warning, of the law. Now when sin presses on the soul to this purpose, it will use a thousand wiles to hide from it the terror of the Lord, the end of transgressions, and especially of the particular folly it is soliciting the mind toward. #emph[Hopes of pardon] will be used to hide it; #emph[future repentance] will hide it; the #emph[present urgency] of lust will hide it; #emph[occasions and opportunities] will hide it; #emph[surprises] will hide it; the #emph[lessening] of sin will hide it; #emph[weighing duties] against it will hide it; #emph[fixing the imagination] on present objects will hide it; #emph[desperate resolutions] to risk the uttermost for the enjoyment of lust in its pleasures and profits will hide it. It has a thousand wiles that cannot be counted.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[Fourthly, having prevailed this far, gilding over the pleasures of sin and hiding its end and what it deserves, it goes on to raise #emph[perverse reasonings] in the mind, to fix it on the sin set before it, so that the sin may be conceived and brought forth, since the affections have already been prevailed on. We will speak of this under the next head of its progress.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[Here we may stop a little, as before, to give a few directions for heading off this woeful work of the deceitfulness of sin. If we would not be #emph[enticed] or #emph[entangled,] if we would not be disposed to the conception of sin, if we would be turned out of the road and way that goes down to death, let us take heed to our #emph[affections.] They are of such great concern in the whole course of our obedience that in the Scripture they are commonly called by the name of the #emph[heart,] as the chief thing God requires in our walking before him. And this is not to be attended to slightly. The wise man says in Prov 4:23: “Guard your heart with all diligence,” or, as in the original, above or before all keepings: “Above all that you guard, guard your heart.” You#super[+] have many keepings that you#super[+] watch over. You#super[+] watch to keep your lives, to keep your estates, to keep your reputations, to keep up your families. But, he says, #emph[above all these keepings] prefer this one; attend to the keeping of the heart, of your affections, so that they are not entangled with sin. There is no safety without it. Save everything else and lose the heart, and all is lost, lost for all eternity. You#super[+] will say, then, “What shall we do? How shall we observe this duty?”]]

#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[First, keep your affections with regard to their #emph[object] in general. The apostle gives this advice in this very case in Col 3. His advice at the beginning of that chapter is meant to direct us to the #emph[mortification of sin,] which he expressly takes up in verse 5: “Put to death, therefore, your members that are on the earth.” Prevent the working and deceit of sin that wars in your members. To prepare us and enable us for this, he gives us that great direction in verse 2: “Set your affections on things above, not on earthly things.” Fix your affections on heavenly things; this will enable you to mortify sin. Fill them with the things that are above; let them be occupied with them, and so let those things have the chief place in them. Things #emph[above] are blessed and suitable objects, fit for our affections and answering to them: God himself in his beauty and glory; the Lord Jesus Christ, who is “altogether lovely, outstanding among ten thousand”\; grace and glory; the mysteries revealed in the gospel; the blessedness promised by it. It is our duty that our affections should be filled, taken up, and possessed with these things, and it is our happiness when they are. If they were, what access could sin have to our souls with its painted pleasures, its sugared poisons, its venomous baits? How we would loathe all its proposals, and say to them, “Be gone!” as to an abominable thing! For what are the vain, passing pleasures of sin compared with the exceeding recompense of reward that is set before us? The apostle presses this argument in 2 Cor 4:18.]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[Secondly, as to the #emph[object of your affections in a special way:] let it be the #emph[cross of Christ,] which has an exceeding efficacy for defeating the whole work of indwelling sin. Gal 6:14: “But as for me, may I never boast, except in the cross of our Lord Jesus Christ, through which the world has been crucified to me, and I to the world.” The #emph[cross of Christ] was what he boasted and rejoiced in. His heart was set on it, and these were its effects: it crucified the world to him; it made the world a dead and undesirable thing. The baits and pleasures of sin are all taken out of the world and the things in the world, that is, “the lust of the flesh, the lust of the eyes, and the pride of life.” These are the things in the world, and from them sin takes all its baits, by which it entices and entangles our souls. If the heart is filled with the #emph[cross of Christ,] it casts death and undesirableness on them all. It leaves no seeming beauty, no apparent pleasure or attractiveness in them. Again, he says, “it crucifies me to the world.” It makes my heart, my affections, my desires dead to all these things. It roots up corrupt lusts and affections, and leaves no principle to go out and make provision for the flesh, to gratify its lusts. So labor to fill your hearts with the #emph[cross of Christ.] Consider the sorrows he underwent, the curse he bore, the blood he shed, the cries he uttered, the love that was in all this toward your souls, and the mystery of the grace of God in it. Meditate on the vileness, the desert, and the punishment of sin as represented in the cross, the blood, and the death of Christ. Is Christ #emph[crucified] for sin, and will our hearts not be #emph[crucified] with him to sin? Shall we welcome that thing, or listen to its flirtations, which wounded, pierced, and slew our dear Lord Jesus? God forbid! Fill your affections with the #emph[cross of Christ,] so that there may be no room for sin. The world once put him out of the house into a stable when he came to save us; let him now turn the world out of doors, now that he has come to sanctify us.]]

#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[Secondly, look to the #emph[vigor of the affections] toward heavenly things. If they are not constantly attended to, roused, directed, and warned, they are apt to decay, and sin lies in wait to take every advantage against them. We have many complaints in the Scripture of those who lost their “first love” by letting their affections decay. This should make us #emph[jealous] over our own hearts, so that we too are not overtaken by the same backsliding frame. So be jealous over them; examine them often and strictly and call them to account; supply them with proper considerations to rouse and stir them up to duty.]
